    'Chorionicity is best determined by ultrasound at 11–14 weeks. A lambda (twin-peak) sign — chorionic tissue extending into the base of a thick inter-twin membrane — indicates dichorionic twins; a thin membrane meeting the placenta at a right angle (T sign) indicates monochorionic diamniotic twins. Monochorionic twins share a placenta with vascular anastomoses, carrying risks of twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome, selective growth restriction, TAPS and co-twin death, so they need fortnightly scans from 16 weeks. Monoamniotic twins risk cord entanglement.',
