    'Antenatal corticosteroids given to women at risk of preterm birth (generally 24–34 weeks, sometimes from 22–23 weeks or up to 36 weeks in selected cases) accelerate fetal lung maturation (surfactant production) and reduce RDS, intraventricular haemorrhage, necrotising enterocolitis and neonatal death; the maximum benefit is 24 hours to 7 days after the first dose. Magnesium sulphate before 32 weeks provides fetal neuroprotection. Tocolysis (nifedipine, atosiban) may delay delivery to allow steroids to act or transfer in utero. Antibiotics are given for PPROM or GBS, not routinely with intact membranes.',
