    'Primary dysmenorrhoea is painful menstruation without pelvic pathology, beginning within 1–2 years of menarche when ovulatory cycles establish. Progesterone withdrawal triggers release of prostaglandins (PGF2α, PGE2) from the endometrium, causing strong uterine contractions, ischaemia and systemic symptoms (nausea, diarrhoea, headache). NSAIDs (mefenamic acid, ibuprofen, naproxen), started at or just before the onset of pain, are first-line; combined hormonal contraceptives are an alternative or addition. Secondary dysmenorrhoea suggests endometriosis, adenomyosis, PID or fibroids.',
