    'Vitamin K deficiency bleeding (haemorrhagic disease of the newborn) occurs because newborns have low vitamin K stores, little placental transfer, sterile gut flora and low vitamin K in breast milk. Late VKDB (2–12 weeks) particularly affects exclusively breastfed babies who did not receive prophylaxis, and those with cholestasis, and often presents with intracranial haemorrhage. Vitamin K-dependent factors (II, VII, IX, X) are low, so PT is prolonged with normal platelets. A single IM dose of vitamin K1 (1 mg; 0.5 mg if under 1 kg) at birth prevents it; treatment is IV vitamin K and fresh frozen plasma.',
