    'IgA vasculitis is the commonest childhood vasculitis: IgA immune complex deposition in small vessels causes palpable purpura (with normal platelets) on the buttocks and extensor surfaces of the legs, arthritis/arthralgia, abdominal pain (with risk of intussusception and GI bleeding) and nephritis (haematuria, proteinuria). It usually resolves within weeks with supportive care; steroids help severe abdominal pain. Renal involvement determines long-term outcome, so urine and blood pressure are monitored for at least 6–12 months, with nephrology referral for significant proteinuria or renal impairment.',
