    'West syndrome consists of infantile spasms (clusters of flexor, extensor or mixed spasms, often on waking), hypsarrhythmia on EEG and developmental arrest or regression, usually starting at 3–12 months. Causes include hypoxic–ischaemic injury, tuberous sclerosis, structural brain malformations, metabolic and genetic disorders. It is an epileptic encephalopathy needing urgent treatment: hormonal therapy (ACTH or high-dose prednisolone) or vigabatrin — vigabatrin is first-line in tuberous sclerosis. Lennox–Gastaut syndrome may follow.',
